Software profiles (data/software/*.json)
- Each record is written by pickfit from public sources: repository facts from the
  GitHub REST API, and short verbatim quotes from the project's own README and
  release notes as evidence for each extracted field. Every record lists the URL of
  each source it quotes. Quoted text remains under its project's license; pickfit
  claims no rights in it.
- Popularity signals in scores are counts read from public APIs: the npm registry
  and api.npmjs.org downloads, the PyPI JSON API and pypistats.org, the crates.io
  API, Homebrew's formulae.brew.sh API, and Hacker News stories through the Algolia
  HN Search API. Only package names, counts and story scores are kept.
- Candidates come from GitHub repository search and from Show HN posts. Show HN posts
  are read through the Algolia HN Search API (hn.algolia.com/api, which limits each
  IP to 10,000 requests an hour) and the official Hacker News API
  (github.com/HackerNews/API, MIT). Of a post, pickfit's local pipeline database keeps
  only its id, HN URL, title, link, points, comment count and time; a record lists
  only the post's URL, as a source of kind "hn". A post's text and comments belong to
  their authors; they are not copied, and no field of a record is taken from them.
- Desktop app candidates also come from Flathub and Homebrew Cask (terms checked in
  docs/source-terms.md). Flathub: the AppStream catalog that flatpak clients download
  (dl.flathub.org/repo/appstream), whose metadata is under the permissive licenses the
  AppStream specification allows; the flathub.org website, its API and its install
  statistics are not used. Homebrew Cask: the cask definitions (Homebrew/homebrew-cask,
  BSD-2-Clause) and cask install counts from formulae.brew.sh, used only to pick
  candidates and never stored. A record's profile still comes from the app's GitHub
  repository; it lists the app's Flathub or cask page as a source.
- Hosted web app candidates (kind web_app) come from the same Show HN APIs and from
  GitHub repositories whose homepage is a website (terms and rules in
  docs/source-terms.md). For each site pickfit reads robots.txt, the home page and one
  terms page as "pickfit/0.1", and leaves out sites whose robots.txt or terms forbid
  automated access. A home page drawn by script is loaded once in headless Chromium
  through Playwright (Apache-2.0; run with `uv run --with playwright`, not a project
  dependency). The page text stays in the local pipeline database only until extraction
  has read it; a record keeps extracted facts with short verbatim quotes, and lists the
  site, the Show HN post and the repository it came from.

